**Proposition 5.2 (Finite coefficient and norm certificate).** At
$p=v=0$ over $\mathbb F_{101}$, the raw leading coefficient is
$\rho=75$. The following arrays give the coefficients in increasing
powers of $x$, including the final monic coefficient of $\bar R$:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\bar R={}&(46,95,81,62,63,79,48,47,53,61,51,37,56,53,49,6,82,8,1),\\
\delta={}&(25,72,55,25,80,25,88,86,100,100,87,31,85,34,18,36,84,8),\\
\tau={}&(6,14,74,79,14,63,53,68,75,51,27,33,52,44,98,31,70,78).
\end{aligned}
\tag{5.4}
$$

The characteristic polynomial $\chi$ has the following array in
decreasing powers of its terminal variable $z$:

$$
\chi=(1,60,34,67,95,86,81,15,46,78,86,25,51,76,54,17,20,41,28).
\tag{5.5}
$$

Its exact determinant data in this field are

$$
\begin{array}{c|r}
\text{quantity}&\text{residue}\\ \hline
\operatorname{Res}_x(\bar R,\partial_x\bar R)&97\\
\operatorname{Disc}_x(\bar R)&4\\
\operatorname{Res}_z(\chi,\partial_z\chi)&71\\
\operatorname{Disc}_z(\chi)&30\\
\kappa&82\\
\operatorname{Res}_x(\bar R,D)&23\\
\operatorname{Res}_x(\bar R,\alpha)&62\\
\operatorname{Res}_x(\bar R,a)&76\\
\operatorname{Res}_x(\bar R,\beta)&70
\end{array}
\tag{5.6}
$$

The source-reconstruction norms are

$$
\begin{array}{c|rrrrrrrrrrr}
u&\delta&\delta-24&H_0&H_1&U&b&j&n&T&r&1-r\\ \hline
\operatorname{Nm}(u)&50&100&43&73&15&53&33&84&13&39&31.
\end{array}
\tag{5.7}
$$

At the separate specialization $p=1,v=2$ over the same field,

$$
S_F(1,2)=\operatorname{Res}_x(F_0,\alpha)=52.
\tag{5.8}
$$

In particular each displayed nonzero scalar certifies a nonzero
characteristic-zero polynomial or rational function defined by the
corresponding circuit.

Proof. All entries follow from the finite definitions (4.1)–(4.7) and
(5.1)–(5.3) with reduction modulo $101$. The following coefficient
rules specify the calculation completely using only finite field
arithmetic. Polynomial products are reduced by monic division by the
first array in (5.4). If $u=(u_0,\ldots,u_{17})$ and
$v=(v_0,\ldots,v_{17})$, form the convolution
$w_\ell=\sum_{i+j=\ell}u_iv_j$, then for each degree
$\ell\ge18$, in decreasing order, subtract $w_\ell x^{\ell-18}\bar R$.
The resulting eighteen entries are the product in the quotient.
Inversion uses the extended Euclidean identity with $\bar R$, or
the adjugate rule of Definition 5.1.

The resultant quotient (4.7), divided by its leading coefficient
$75$, gives the first array. Applying $-\beta/\alpha$ and then the
four linear forms $a_i/D$ gives the second and third arrays. For
example every denominator in these operations has nonzero norm:
$\operatorname{Nm}(\alpha)=62$ and $\operatorname{Nm}(D)=23$.
The source divisions additionally use the norms $50$ for $\delta$
and $73$ for $H_1$. The constants $2,5$ are units.

For an explicit scalar recurrence giving (5.5), let
$s_i=\operatorname{tr}(M(\tau^i))$, set $c_0=1$, and for
$1\le k\le18$ set

$$
c_k=-k^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^k c_{k-i}s_i
\quad\hbox{in }\mathbb F_{101}.
\tag{5.9}
$$

All $k$ here are units. Newton's identities give the descending
characteristic coefficients $c_0,\ldots,c_{18}$, which are exactly
(5.5). The determinant of the eighteen successively reduced columns
$1,\tau,\ldots,\tau^{17}$ is $82$. The Sylvester determinants of
the two monic degree-eighteen polynomials and their derivatives are
$97$ and $71$. Since $(-1)^{18\cdot17/2}=-1$, their discriminants
are $4$ and $30$. The identity

$$
30=4\cdot82^2\pmod {101}
\tag{5.10}
$$

also agrees with the Vandermonde change of basis. Applying the same
multiplication determinant to the eleven explicitly reconstructed
elements gives (5.7). Reduction gives zero for each of

$$
\begin{gathered}
f(x,\delta),\quad K(x,\delta),\\
(294/5-2\delta-H_2)U-H_2,\\
r^2-(1+b)r+b(n-b).
\end{gathered}
\tag{5.11}
$$

Here $294/5=79$ in $\mathbb F_{101}$. These last zero residues
are finite identities in this specialization; the universal identities
needed in characteristic zero are proved in Theorem 6.1 below.

For (5.8), substitute $(1,2)$ in the polynomial $\alpha$ of (4.5)
and take its Sylvester determinant with the cubic $F_0$; it is $52$.
No rational inversion is involved in this separate resultant. It is
necessary to distinguish this specialization: at $(0,0)$, $F_0$
has roots $-1,-2$, so that point is not asserted to belong to the
final generic base open.

Finally all the circuits before reduction have integer coefficients,
apart from rational constants and the explicitly prescribed inverses.
The leading coefficient and all inversion norms are nonzero modulo
$101$. Thus their denominators are units at this specialization.
A rational function identically zero in characteristic zero would
have zero residue at every specialization where its defining circuit
has unit denominators. The nonzero residues therefore prove the
stated characteristic-zero nonidentities. This inference uses
nonvanishing residues; it does not lift the modular zero identities
of (5.11) to universal identities. ∎

